    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded during the Gold Rush, the town began as a tent city in 1848 before a church was built.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Andreas%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mexican gold miners first settled the area in 1848, naming the village after the local Catholic parish of St. Andrew.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Andreas%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A gold nugget discovered in August 1852 was sold to Wells Fargo, sparking the town’s early boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Andreas%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1853 underground river gold revitalized San Andreas, turning it from a fleeting camp into a permanent town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Andreas%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "By 1866 San Andreas had become the seat of Calaveras County, cementing its regional importance.",
